.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

int server_accept_client(int server_fd);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Accepts a new client connection on the listening Unix domain socket.
Calls \f[CR]accept()\f[R] on \f[CR]server_fd\f[R] and sets the
\f[CR]FD_CLOEXEC\f[R] flag on the returned client file descriptor to
prevent leaking it to child processes spawned by bash command execution.
.SH PARAMETERS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]server_fd\f[R] \(en The listening socket file descriptor, as
returned by \f[CR]server_socket_create()\f[R].
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the client file descriptor on success (a non\-negative integer).
.PP
Returns \f[CR]\-1\f[R] on error, with \f[CR]errno\f[R] set
appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]EINTR\f[R] \(en The call was interrupted by a signal (typically
\f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] or \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R]).
The main accept loop checks \f[CR]server_running\f[R] and retries.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ECONNABORTED\f[R] \(en Connection aborted (on Cygwin, this can
occur due to the \f[CR]SO_PEERCRED\f[R] handshake race; see
\f[CR]server_socket_create\f[R](3)).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]EMFILE\f[R] / \f[B]ENFILE\f[R] \(en Too many open file descriptors.
.IP \(bu 2
Standard \f[CR]accept()\f[R] errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]server_socket_create\f[R](3), \f[CR]session_init\f[R](3),
\f[CR]accept\f[R](2), \f[CR]fcntl\f[R](2)
.SH NOTES
The \f[CR]FD_CLOEXEC\f[R] flag is set via
\f[CR]fcntl(client_fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC)\f[R] immediately after
accept.
This ensures the client socket is not inherited by child processes
created during \f[CR]parse_and_execute()\f[R].
.PP
The caller is responsible for closing the returned file descriptor when
the session ends, typically via \f[CR]session_cleanup()\f[R].
.PP
In the main server loop, \f[CR]EINTR\f[R] is treated as a non\-fatal
condition.
When a signal sets \f[CR]server_running = 0\f[R], the loop exits
naturally after the interrupted \f[CR]accept()\f[R] returns.
.PP
The function uses \f[CR]struct sockaddr_storage\f[R] for the client
address, although for \f[CR]AF_UNIX\f[R] sockets the address is not used
after accept.
